# Enhanced Stability of Perovskite Solar Cells with Low-Temperature Hydrothermally Grown SnO <sub/>2</sub> Electron Transport Layers

## abstract
Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) may offer huge potential in photovoltaic conversion, yet their practical applications face one major obstacle: their low stability, or quick degradation of their initial efficiencies. Here, a new design scheme is presented to enhance the PSC stability by using low-temperature hydrothermally grown hierarchical nano-SnO2 electron transport layers (ETLs). The ETL contains a thin compact SnO2 layer underneath a mesoporous layer of SnO2 nanosheets. The mesoporous layer plays multiple roles of enhancing photon collection, preventing moisture penetration and improving the long-term stability. Through such simple approaches, PSCs with power conversion efficiencies of ≈13% can be readily obtained, with the highest efficiency to be 16.17%. A prototypical PSC preserves 90% of its initial efficiency even after storage in air at room temperature for 130 d without encapsulation. This study demonstrates that hierarchical SnO2 is a potential ETL for fabricating low-cost and efficient PSCs with long-term stability.

Enhanced Stability of Perovskite Solar Cells with Low-Temperature Hydrothermally Grown $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ Electron Transport Layers
Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) may offer huge potential in photovoltaic conversion, yet their practical applications face one major obstacle: their low stability, or quick degradation of their initial efficiencies. Here, a new design scheme is presented to enhance the PSC stability by using low-temperature hydrothermally grown hierarchical nano- $\cdot\mathsf{s n O}_{2}$ electron transport layers (ETLs). The ETL contains a thin compact $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ layer underneath a mesoporous layer of $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ nanosheets. The mesoporous layer plays multiple roles of enhancing photon collection, preventing moisture penetration and improving the long-term stability. Through such simple approaches, PSCs with power conversion efficiencies of $\approx13\%$ can be readily obtained, with the highest efficiency to be $16.17\%$ . A prototypical PSC preserves $90\%$ of its initial efficiency even after storage in air at room temperature for 130 d without encapsulation. This study demonstrates that hierarchical $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ is a potential ETL for fabricating low-cost and efficient PSCs with long-term stability.
## 1. Introduction
Organic–inorganic lead halide perovskite $\mathrm{(CH_{3}N H_{3}P b X_{3}}$ , $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\Sigma}}\times=\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\Sigma}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I\Sigma}}$ , Br, and Cl) solar cells (PSCs) have gathered tremendous attention not only due to their excellent power conversion efficiency (PCE) but also because of the low cost of the materials involved. The performance of the PSCs has been greatly improved through various innovative optimization approaches in the device structures[1–7] and the related materials.[8–17] In the architecture of most solar cells including PSCs, the electron transport layer (ETL) is an indispensable component, which plays the dual roles of transporting electrons and blocking holes. Therefore, proper choice of the ETL is vital in improving the overall performance of the PSCs.
To date, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ has been the most common ETL material in the PSCs, pushing the PCE to the record high of $22.1\%$ through various material and structure optimizations.[9,11,16,18–22] One major disadvantage of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ -based ETL is the high processing temperatures, which necessarily translates into high costs while also impeding the development of flexible PSCs. Another topic we should concern is the long-term stability of PSCs. Various approaches have been made in overcoming the instability of PSCs,[23–27] such as materials engineering strategies of perovskite layers and hole transport layers (HTLs), and inserting protecting interfacial layers.[28–31] Optimizing and designing the structures and relative interfaces of ETLs are beneficial to enhance device stability.[25,30] $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ is a frequently used ETL material,[32–34] which does not need hightemperature processing, and the $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ -based PSCs have exhibited PCEs as high as ${\approx}15.7\%$ .[34] Nevertheless, $z\mathrm{nO}$ is very sensitive to weak acids or alkali, a harmful factor to the long-term stability of the PSCs.[32,35] Hence, some other suitable n-type ETL materials have gradually came into researcher’s sight. Starting from last year, $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ has been increasingly exploited as the third candidate ETL material in PSCs while most of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL fabrications require high-temperature annealing treatment or a vacuum condition.[36–40] We reported efficient PSCs based on sol–gel derived $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL under $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ of heat treatment.[39] This temperature is still too high for future largearea flexible photovoltaic (PV) fabrications and the device longterm stability is also an important issue for PV applications. The advantages of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ are manifold, including low chemical reactivity and photocatalytic activity,[41] a wider band gap (than $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{znO}^{\prime}$ ), higher electron mobility, and potential low processing temperatures. Given its inherently excellent properties, it is natural to expect overall better long-term performance from such $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based PSCs, a critical aspect which so far has not been explored but is the focus of the present study. In doing so, we integrate the intrinsic superior properties of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ with the added advantages due to the introduction of a mesoporous structure.
In this work, we present a new design scheme to enhance the long-term stability of the PSC by using low-temperature hydrothermally grown hierarchical nano- $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{S}}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{n}}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{O}}_{2}$ ETLs. The hierarchical $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL, consisting of a thin compact layer and a mesoporous layer, is demonstrated to be able to enhance photon collection, prevent moisture penetration, and improve the long-term stability. It is also notable that, using such simple low-temperature approaches (typically below $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ ), PSCs with PCEs of ${\approx}13\%$ can be readily obtained in this first study, with the highest PCE already reaching $16.17\%$ . More significant, a prototypical PSC is shown to preserve $90\%$ of its initial efficiency even after $3000\mathrm{{h}}$ without encapsulation. The present study demonstrates that hierarchical $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ is a potential ETL for developing low-cost and large-area flexible PSCs with high efficiency and long-term stability.
## 2. Results and Discussion
Figure 1a shows the schematic view of the PSC configuration: including the FTO front contact, hierarchical $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL, $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ absorber layer, spiro-OMeTAD HTL, and Au electrode. The corresponding diagram of band energy level is shown in Figure S1 (Supporting Information). Figure 1b shows the cross-sectional image of a complete PSC. It is noted that the hierarchical $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL is composed of the mesoporous layer of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanosheet arrays that are largely vertically aligned on the compact layer of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticles. Here, the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL plays a crucial role in the PSCs: The compact layer can inhibit the recombination of the photogenerated electrons and holes, while the nanosheet arrays can accelerate electron transport from the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ film to the FTO front contact, provide with more contact specific area with $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ and also contribute to suppressing the charge recombination. In addition, the mesoporous $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanosheets as the scaffold are beneficial to absorb and fix the mutually intercalated perovskite so that the PSC can absorb light more efficiently. It is noted that the hierarchical structure of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL can be readily formed by a one-step hydrothermal approach at a low temperature of $95~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
We carried out a systematic study to illustrate the growth mechanism of the mesoscopic $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and explore the effects of growth time on the morphological change of the hierarchical $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs. Figure 2a displays the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ growth process after hydrothermal reaction for 0 (bare FTO), 2, 3, 4, 5, and $^\textrm{\scriptsize6h}$ , respectively. Figure 2b shows the corresponding scanning electron microscope (SEM) images of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL morphologies. At the beginning of the reaction, there were some $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticles grown on the surface of the FTO substrates. After a $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ hydrothermal reaction, a compact layer (or blocking layer) composed of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticles was formed, covering the large FTO crystals (Figure 2a,b for 2h). With increasing reaction time, some discontinuous $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanosheets emerged on the compact $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticle layer (Figure 2,b for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ ). An increasing number of larger and thicker $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanosheets was grown with longer reaction time, eventually a continuous $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanosheet network was obtained. We found that the optimum growth time is $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ (Figure 2a,b for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ ). When appropriate density and hight of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanosheets were obtained, a homogeneous mesoporous layer was formed. The cross-sectional SEM image in Figure S2b (Supporting Information) illustrates that the thickness of the FTO film is about $360\mathrm{nm}$ while that of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL with a $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ growth time is about $70\mathrm{nm}$ . The $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanosheets would become excessively dense if the growth time were too long (Figure 2a,b for 5 and 6 h). Larger and thicker nanosheets were obtained with further growth time such as $5\mathrm{-}10\mathrm{~h~}$ . The higher magnification SEM image shown in the inset of Figure S2a (Supporting Information) for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ illustrates that the nanosheets are themselves composed of nanoparticles. The sectional SEM picture in Figure S2b (Supporting Information) illustrates that the thickness of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL for $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ growth time is ${\approx}250~\mathrm{nm}$ and nanosheets composed of nanoparticles can be clearly seen.
X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern of our hydrothermally processed hierarchical $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ on FTO is similar to that of the FTO substrate without the emergence of any other impure phase (Figure S3, Supporting Information). High-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) pictures of the hydrothermal-grown $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , as shown in Figure S4 (Supporting Information), indicate that the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ is polycrystalline. In addition, the selective area electron diffraction image (Figure S4c, Supporting Information) also confirms that the hydrothermally synthesized $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ is polycrystalline although there may be some amorphous $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ . X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra revealed that the composition of the films prepared by the $\mathrm{Sn}(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4})$ precursor is $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ . The full survey XPS spectrum is shown in Figure S5 (Supporting Information), which is in agreement with the literature.[39]
The obtained typical hierarchical structure may be beneficial to the subsequent deposition of the perovskite absorber. Specifically, the unique structure can provide the needed large volume for $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}$ solutions to be filled in, help to inhibit swelling and contraction of the perovskite materials during annealing, and prevent void formation within the perovskite material and its electromigration degradation.[42]
## 2.1. Perovskite Photovoltaic Performance
We found that the growth time of the mesoscopic $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ in the laboratory oven plays an important role in determining the performance of the mesoporous PSCs. Figure 3a, Figure $\mathtt{S7a}$ and Table S1 (Supporting Information) show the $J{-}V$ curves and PV parameters of the PSCs with various growth times of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ on the FTO substrates measured under reverse voltage scanning. From Figure 3a and Figure S7a (Supporting Information), it is seen that the performance of the PSCs with $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based ETLs is much better than that of the PSCs without an $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL $(0\ \mathrm{h})$ . For the solar cells whose $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs were grown in $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the performance is better than that without $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL but still mediocre. The hydrothermal reaction time was too short for the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanosheets to grow, but there were already enough $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticles to form a compact (or called planar) $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ layer. This compact layer is conducive to blocking the holes and reducing the charge recombination, therefore, the device with a 2 h $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL have a better performance. With the increase of the growth time, an increasing number of nanosheets were formed, which in turn contributed to extracting and transferring the electrons more quickly. The optimum growth time for the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL is about $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ and a mediate density of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanosheets is appropriate. The performance of the PSC with 4 h $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL has a PCE of $15.29\%$ , an open-circuit voltage $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.03\mathrm{V},$ a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $21.66\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and an FF of 0.689 measured under reverse voltage scanning. The $J{-}V$ curves of the PSC based on $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ hierarchical $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL measured under reverse and forward scans are also shown in Figure S7d (Supporting Information). The $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ grown $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL is a mesoporous structure which contributes to the absorption of perovskite material, comparing with the hydrothermally grown planar $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL (2 h). This speculation was demonstrated by the ultraviolet– visible absorption spectra displayed in Figure S6 (Supporting Information). The perovskite layer based on 4 h $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL presents the best light absorption, contributing to the performance enhancement of the PSCs. This is also confirmed by the incident photon-to-current conversion efficiency (IPCE) results shown in Figure 3c, which are basically consistent with the trend of $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ in the $J{-}V$ curves as shown in Figure 3a. The calculated integration $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of the PSC based on 4 h $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL is $19.54~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (Figure S8, Supporting Information), which is acceptable considering that the measured $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ is obtained under reverse voltage scanning.
To illustrate the charge transfer between different layers in the device, we conducted the steady-state photoluminescence (PL) measurements with configurations of FTO/ETL/ $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ or $\mathrm{FTO/CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ (Figure 3b and Figure S7c, Supporting Information). It is seen from the PL spectra that FTO/Perovskite structure exhibited the highest intensity while the PL intensity of the perovskite based on the $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ hierarchical $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL is the lowest. These results suggest that $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ hierarchical $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs can extract and transfer the electrons from the perovskite layer quickly, contributing to the increment of the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ . From Table S1 (Supporting Information), the devices (with 2, 3, and 4 ETLs) have more or less the same $V_{\mathrm{OC}}.$ , which indicates that recombination is not a major issue in compact and hierarchical films. The lower reverse currents in dark
$J{-}V$ curves of PSCs based on $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL, comparing with that on bare FTO, confirm the better hole blocking ability in the devices when a thin layer of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ has been grown on FTO (Figure S9, Supporting Information). Therefore, the fill factor of the devices with $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs has a big increment comparing with that without ETLs.
A highly transparent ETL layer is also essential for an efficient PSC. Transmission spectra of the pure FTO glass, a $60\ \mathrm{nm}$ compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film, and the mesoporous hierarchical $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films with different growth times coated on FTO glasses are shown in Figure 3d. Compared with the compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film, the optical absorption edges of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films shift to shorter wavelengths and much better transmittance is gained. In addition, we observe that the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film on FTO with the growth time of $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ is antireflective, and the transparency of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films on FTO with longer growth times is still satisfactory.
It is also notable that the low-temperature $(95~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ solution process makes it possible to fabricate PSCs based on polyethylene terephthalate (PET)/ITO flexible substrates, achieving a PCE of $8.78\%$ (Figure S7b, Supporting Information). The relatively larger sheet resistance of the ITO thin film, the low transmittance of the PET substrates, and the difficulty for obtaining homogeneous film on PET substrates are all responsible for the poor performance of the PSC. It is anticipated that the performance of the flexible PSC based on hierarchical $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL can be substantially improved through future optimization efforts.
## 2.2. Perovskite Photovoltaic Stability
For the $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ grown cells, we got a champion cell with a PCE of $16.17\%$ , a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.05~\mathrm{V},$ a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $22.76~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}$ , and an FF of 0.680 measured under reverse voltage scanning (Figure S10, Supporting Information). We put the cell in lab condition $(\approx70\%$ humidity) without any encapsulation. Surprisingly, after $20\textup{d}$ , we found the champion cell still preserves $73\%$ of its initial efficiency, and $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ scarcely changes even after 480 hours at this high humidity (Figure S10, Supporting Information).
Encouraged by this surprising observation, we carried out a comparative study of 5 days’ stability measurement for the PSCs with hierarchical $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs versus compact $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs and without ETLs under $70\%$ humidity. The results are shown in Figure $4\mathrm{a-c}$ . It illustrates that PSCs using hierarchical $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs has a better moisture-stability comparing with planar ones. The results demonstrate that PSC’s moisture tolerance can be dramatically improved by using hierarchical $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs.
To gain further insight into the effect of the hierarchical $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL on the PSCs, we utilized XRD measurements to illustrate the crystallinity evolutions of the perovskite based on different structure ETL under a low humidity $(\approx20\%)$ and a high humidity $(\approx95\%)$ (Figure 5). The main diffraction peaks centered at $14.2^{\circ}$ and $28.5^{\circ}$ are attributed to the $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ , the peaks centered at $12.7^{\circ}$ , $25.6^{\circ}$ , and $38.7^{\circ}$ can be assigned to $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , and the peak centered at $10.6^{\circ}$ corresponds to the $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}(\mathrm{DMSO})_{2}$ . For the perovskite based on the compact $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL ( $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ grown), we found that both diffraction patterns from $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}(\mathrm{DMSO})_{2}$ are present after $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ under $95\%$ humidity, an obvious peak of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ is present after $1500\mathrm{h}$ under $20\%$ humidity. On the contrary, almost no changes occurred for the perovskite/hierarchical $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL after $1500\mathrm{~h~}$ under $20\%$ humidity, and only a small peak of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ is present after a $10\mathrm{~h~}$ exposure to moisture under $95\%$ humidity. These results demonstrate that the hierarchical $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL has a positive effect on preventing the infiltration of the moisture and perovskite phase separation.
Inspired by these results, we select samples which ${\approx}13\%$ PCE can be readily obtained and carried out more systematic stability measurements of the PSCs with $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs for $130\mathrm{d}$ or over $3000\mathrm{~h~}$ . Figure 4d presents the results of the long-term stability tests of the device and Figure S11 (Supporting Information) shows the steady-state efficiency of the cell. Without being encapsulated, the tested devices were stored in a drying cupboard where humidity and temperature were about $20\%$ and $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , respectively. The values of $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ and PCE first increased and then decreased slightly. The initial increase of the PCE may be owing to the diffusion of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into the Spiro-OMeTAD, which has been reported in the literature.[43,44] It is interesting that there was a slight increase in $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ while the FF remained almost unchanged for a long period. Therefore, the PCE of the device was above $90\%$ of its initial value after $3000\mathrm{{h}}$ . Unfortunately, the Au electrode of the PSC has been mechanically damaged after testing for many times and we believe that the device would still maintain good performance for much longer time if it were well preserved.
Our previous work demonstrated that hierarchical porous structures are beneficial to carrier transport and stability improvements for PV devices.[45,46] The enhanced stability may be related to photoelectrochemical, electromechanical, and/ or electromigration processes.[42,47,48] Therefore, the chemical composition, crystal quality, and mechanical property control of the perovskite absorber layer are very important for the PSCs.
The hybrid network of mesoporous $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanosheets and covalently cross-linked hydrophilic perovskite absorber may form a hierarchical structure with robust mechanical and chemical stability. The hierarchical structure provides efficient electron diffusion through the network of interconnected $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanosheets and perovskite crystallites. This is also proven by the spectroscopic measurements (Figure 3b). The hierarchical $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ layer can not only provide an excellent charge transport pathway but also inhibit the damage of the perovskite material by moisture in air,[49] so that the PV performance of the PSCs can be maintained for a very long time in ambient air.
## 3. Conclusions
In summary, we have demonstrated that one-step and lowtemperature hydrothermal-processed hierarchical $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ can serve as effective ETLs in improving the overall long-term performance of the PSCs. The underlying reasons for its success are twofold. First, $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ is intrinsically a wide band gap material, and has high electron mobility and low chemical and photocatalytic reactivity. Second, the hierarchical structure is able to enhance light absorption of the perovskite, prevent degradation or phase separation of the perovskite material from moisture in the air, facilitate the transport of photoelectrons, and improve the overall stability of the devices. Collectively, the central findings presented here establish hierarchical $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ to be an alternative candidate as the choice material design of ETL with immense potentials for developing low-cost flexible PSCs with high efficiency, long-term stability, and versatile practical applications.
## 4. Experimental Section
Fabrication of Hydrothermally Grown Compact $S n O_{2}\ E T L$ or Hierarchical $S n O_{2}$ ETL: Hexamethylenetetramine $(C_{6}\mathsf{H}_{12}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ , $M=740.79\textrm{g}\mathsf{m o l}^{-1}$ , Aladdin, $99.0\%$ ) and tin oxalate powder $(\mathsf{S n}(\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4})$ , $M=206.73\ \mathrm{g}\ m o l^{-1}$ , Aladdin) were added and dissolved in $50~\mathsf{m l}$ deionized (DI) water at a concentration of $0.025~\mathrm{~M~}$ (molar ratio: 1:1) inside a $700~\mathrm{{mL}}$ white mouth bottle. Then, the mixture was stirred under ambient conditions for an hour. Before dipping into the $50~\mathrm{mL}$ nutrient solution, FTO glass substrates (Asahi Glass, 14 Ohm/square) were sequentially rinsed by sonication in detergent, DI water, acetone and ethanol, and finally dried in air. The hydrothermal growth was conducted inside a laboratory oven at $95~^{\circ}C$ for a certain time from 0 to $\mathsf{\Delta}\mathsf{l}0\mathsf{h}$ . After the growth had finished, the laboratory oven was cooled down to room temperature in ambient air. Then the samples were taken out, rinsed by DI water for several minutes, and finally dried in air.
Solar Cell Fabrication: At first, the FTO glass substrates with $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ ETL were treated with ultraviolet ozone for $30~\mathsf{m i n}$ . The perovskite ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ film was deposited by the sequential deposition method.[17] Then, 1 m $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ in dimethylformamide and dimethylsulfoxide (DMSO) was spin-coated on the FTO substrates with $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ ETLs and dried for several minutes in air. Second, the substrates were dipped into $10~\mathsf{m g}\mathsf{m L^{-1}}\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{N H}_{3}\mathsf{I}$ dissolved in isopropanol. Finally, the films annealed in ambient air at $70~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 40 min. After the formation of perovskite film, the hole transport material contained $68\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~M~}$ spiroOMeTAD, $55\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~M~TBP}$ , and $26\times10^{-3}~\mathrm{{\textperthousand}}$ Li-TFSI in acetonitrile and chlorobenzene (1:10 in volume ratio), and was spin-coated on the perovskite film. A thin layer of gold was finally deposited by thermal evaporation as the electrode.
Characterization: The morphology of the $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ films and the PSCs were observed by a high-resolution field emission SEM (JSM 6700F). The components of the $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ films were measured by an energy dispersive X-ray spectrometry. The transmission spectra of the ETLs on FTO were measured by an ultraviolet–visible spectrophotometer (CARY5000, Varian) in a wavelength range at $300{-}800~\mathsf{n m}$ at room temperature. XPS was carried out with a monochromatic source (Al-Kα) and ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) was evaluated by using He I radiation at $21.22\:\mathrm{eV}$ from a discharge lamp, which they were recorded on an XPS/UPS system (Thermo Scientific, ESCLAB 250Xi, USA). PL spectra was obtained with a $532~\mathsf{n m}$ laser, as the excitation source, pulsed at a frequency of $9.743~\mathsf{M H z}$ . The $J{-}V$ characteristics of the solar cells were measured by a CHI660D electrochemical workstation (Shang Hai, China) under a $\mathsf{l o o}\mathsf{m w}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ (AM 1.5 simulated irradiation) illumination with a standard ABET Sun 2000 Solar Simulator. A standard silicon solar cell was used to calibrate the light intensity. The scan rate was $0.1\ \vee\ \mathsf{s}^{-1}$ and the active area of all devices was $0.09\ \mathsf{c m}^{2}$ . IPCE was measured by a QE/IPCE system (Newport) in the $370{-}800\ \mathsf{n m}$ wavelength range at room temperature.
